CAT = "nzsecForce"; SEG = "FRC"
Q = [
("Compared with any member of the public, a licensed security guard's legal powers are:",
 'The same', ['Those of a constable', 'Wider on licensed premises', 'Wider while in uniform'],
 'Security staff have no Police powers. In New Zealand law they have the same powers as any member of the public.'),
('Force used by a security officer must be reasonable, necessary and:',
 'Proportionate', ['Pre-approved', 'Witnessed', 'Recorded on video'],
 'Skill standard 40145 states lawful force as reasonable, necessary and proportionate. Recording helps later, but it is not part of the legal test.'),
('Under the Crimes Act, self-defence is judged on the force that is reasonable:',
 'As you believed the facts to be', ['Only if you were struck first', 'Only if a witness later confirms it', 'Only inside your own workplace'],
 'Section 48 justifies reasonable force in defence of yourself or another in the circumstances as you believe them to be. There is no rule that you must be hit first.'),
('Can a guard use force to defend a patron who is being attacked?',
 'Yes, to defend another', ['No, only to defend yourself', 'Only once Police are called', 'Only if the patron asks'],
 'Section 48 covers the defence of yourself or another. The force must still be reasonable in the circumstances.'),
('A guard punches a man who has already stopped resisting. The guard:',
 'Is liable for the excess', ['Is protected by self-defence', 'Is protected by the COA', 'Is protected if the man swore'],
 'Section 62 makes anyone authorised to use force criminally responsible for any excess. Once the threat has ended, more force is no longer justified.'),
('Which section of the Crimes Act deals with excessive force?',
 'Section 62', ['Section 48', 'Section 56', 'Section 42'],
 'Section 62 covers excess force. Section 48 is self-defence, 56 is force against trespassers and 42 is breach of the peace.'),
('Which Crimes Act section covers using force to remove a trespasser?',
 'Section 56', ['Section 52', 'Section 34', 'Section 41'],
 'Section 56 covers force to prevent trespass or remove a person. Section 52 deals with retaking movable property.'),
('Which Crimes Act section deals with retaking stolen property?',
 'Section 52', ['Section 56', 'Section 48', 'Section 62'],
 'Section 52 covers using force to defend movable property and take it back from someone who has taken it.'),
('A person who sees a breach of the peace may, under section 42:',
 'Step in and detain for Police', ['Hold them for up to 24 hours', 'Search their pockets and bag', 'Issue them an infringement notice'],
 'Section 42 allows anyone who witnesses a breach of the peace to interfere to stop it continuing or being renewed and to detain the person to hand them to a constable.'),
('A constable at your venue calls on you to help restrain an offender. The law:',
 'Protects you in assisting', ['Forbids guards to assist', 'Requires a written request', 'Applies only to off-duty guards'],
 "The Crimes Act justifies people who assist a constable when called upon. The NZQA standard lists this (section 34) among a guard's legal powers."),
('Section 41 of the Crimes Act allows reasonable force to prevent:',
 'Suicide or certain serious crimes', ["Any breach of the venue's house rules", 'Patrons leaving without paying', 'People filming the door staff'],
 'Section 41 justifies reasonable force to prevent suicide and some serious offences. It is not a general power to enforce venue rules.'),
('Is a guard legally required to arrest a shoplifter caught in the act?',
 'No, it is a choice', ['Yes, always', 'Yes, if the goods are valuable', 'Yes, if the store asks'],
 'Even where an arrest is allowed, nothing in law requires one. Police advise the safest course is often to call 111, observe and let Police respond.'),
("After making a citizen's arrest, you must contact Police:",
 'As soon as practicable', ['Within 24 hours', 'Only if the person resists', 'After the shift report is done'],
 "Police guidance on citizen's arrest says to call 111 as soon as practicable and follow the directions Police give."),
("After a citizen's arrest, Police advise that you do NOT:",
 'Question the person', ['Check their welfare', 'Call 111 for Police', 'Follow Police directions'],
 'Police say an arrester should not question or search the person, should check their welfare regularly and should call 111 and follow directions.'),
('Which hold do Police warn against when detaining someone?',
 'Kneeling on their back', ['Holding them by the arm', 'Standing beside them', 'Guiding them to a seat'],
 'Police advise never kneeling or sitting on a detained person or pressing on the neck or head, and checking their welfare regularly.'),
('When you arrest someone, you should tell them:',
 'They are held for Police', ['Nothing until Police arrive', 'To empty their pockets', 'To sign a confession'],
 'Police say to tell the person clearly that they are being arrested and held for Police. Anyone arrested has the right to know why.'),
("Any force used in making a citizen's arrest must be:",
 'Reasonable in the circumstances', ['Approved by a duty manager first', 'Witnessed by two other people', 'Reasonable to the person arrested'],
 "NZ Police say to avoid force and, if it is needed, to use only the minimum that is necessary and proportionate. What counts as reasonable depends on the circumstances, not on the arrested person's view, and excessive force can lead to criminal or civil action."),
('Under the Crimes Act, assault can be committed without touching anyone by:',
 'Threatening force by gesture', ['Asking someone to leave the venue', 'Refusing someone entry', 'Writing an incident report'],
 'Assault covers applying force and also threatening force by an act or gesture, so placing someone in fear of immediate force can be an assault.'),
("A guard grabs a patron's phone to stop them filming. This risks a charge of:",
 'Assault', ['Breach of the peace', 'Trespass', 'Disorderly behaviour'],
 'The NZSA guidelines warn that seizing a phone exposes the guard to assault allegations and possibly a property offence. Filming in public cannot be stopped.'),
('Can a guard lawfully stop a member of the public filming them in a public place?',
 'No, only ask them to stop', ['Yes, by taking the phone', 'Yes, by deleting the footage', 'Yes, by detaining the person'],
 'Staff can ask the person not to film, but the request cannot be enforced. Individuals have no privacy right in their image in public.'),
('Detaining someone for a minor breach of venue rules risks:',
 'Unlawful detention', ['A trespass notice', 'A licence upgrade', 'A health and safety fine'],
 'Guards have no power to hold people for breaking house rules. Holding someone without legal justification can be false imprisonment.'),
('The safest first response to an armed robber in a shop is usually to:',
 'Stay back, observe, call 111', ['Tackle them before they get away', 'Block the doorway to trap them', 'Chase them to the car'],
 'Police advise that the safest course is usually to call 111, observe and let Police respond. Guards are not expected to risk injury.'),
('A person refuses to leave after being warned. A guard may then:',
 'Use reasonable force to remove them', ['Use any force needed to remove them', 'Hold them in a back room for Police', 'Take their wallet until they go'],
 'Section 56 covers reasonable force to remove a trespasser. Holding the person or taking property goes beyond removing them.'),
('Before using force to remove someone, good practice is to:',
 'Ask them to leave first', ['Warn them only once Police arrive', 'Take hold of them at once', 'Wait until they become violent'],
 'A person becomes a trespasser once asked to leave and refusing. Asking first also gives them the chance to go without any force.'),
('A guard who has used force on a patron should next:',
 'Report it', ['Delete the CCTV clip', 'Leave it out of the log', 'Wait to see if they complain'],
 "Any use of force is an incident to report under the employer's instructions. CCTV of the event should be kept, not deleted."),
('Which of these is a lawful reason to use force at a venue door?',
 'Defending a colleague under attack', ['Getting a patron to take off a hat', 'Taking a drink off a patron', 'Making a patron give you their name'],
 'Self-defence and defence of others are lawful grounds. House rules on hats, drinks or names do not justify force.'),
('The Crimes Amendment Act 2026 made clear that reasonable force may be used when:',
 'Defending property', ['Enforcing a dress code', 'Collecting an unpaid bill', 'Questioning a suspect'],
 "The Act amended the arrest and defence of property provisions to make clear that reasonable force may be used when making a citizen's arrest and defending property."),
('A patron swears at a guard but makes no threat. Using force against them is:',
 'Not justified', ['Justified as self-defence', 'Justified to keep order', 'Justified if others watch'],
 'Words alone that carry no threat of force do not justify using force. The guard can ask the person to leave and, if they refuse, treat them as a trespasser.'),
]
